APS-014 — Policy Conflict Resolution

Document ID: APS-014
Title: Policy Conflict Resolution
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Policy Conflict Resolution Model (CPCRM) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Policy Conflict Resolution Model establishes the principles, mechanisms, governance, and procedures required to identify, classify, prioritize, and resolve conflicts among Canonical Policy Objects while preserving determinism, explainability, governance integrity, and engineering intent.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Conflict architecture
Conflict detection
Conflict classification
Resolution strategies
Policy precedence
Escalation
Conflict governance
Conflict traceability

This specification applies to every interaction involving two or more Canonical Policy Objects.

3. Canonical Definition

A Policy Conflict is a governed condition in which two or more Canonical Policy Objects produce incompatible or ambiguous outcomes for the same evaluation context.

Conflict resolution removes ambiguity.

It SHALL NOT redefine policy semantics.

4. Objectives

The Policy Conflict Resolution Model SHALL:

Detect conflicts.
Classify conflicts.
Produce deterministic outcomes.
Preserve policy integrity.
Support explainability.
Preserve traceability.
Enable governance.
Support federation.
5. Conflict Resolution Principles

Every conflict resolution SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Explicit Detection

Policy conflicts SHALL be explicitly detected.

Silent conflicts SHALL NOT exist.

Deterministic Resolution

Equivalent conflicts SHALL always produce equivalent resolutions.

Semantic Preservation

Conflict resolution SHALL preserve the semantics of participating policies.

Explainability

Every resolved conflict SHALL include a complete explanation.

Governance

Conflict resolution SHALL follow approved governance rules.

Auditability

Conflict history SHALL remain permanently reconstructable.

6. Conflict Categories

Atlas defines the following canonical conflict categories.

Category	Description
Effect Conflict	Opposing policy effects (Permit vs Deny)
Scope Conflict	Overlapping applicability
Priority Conflict	Equal precedence
Version Conflict	Incompatible policy versions
Dependency Conflict	Unsatisfied dependencies
Temporal Conflict	Conflicting effective periods
Governance Conflict	Different governing authorities
Obligation Conflict	Contradictory obligations

Additional conflict categories MAY be introduced through future specifications.

7. Conflict Detection

Conflict detection SHALL evaluate:

Policy applicability
Policy effects
Policy priorities
Policy versions
Dependencies
Constraints
Effective periods
Governance domains

Detected conflicts SHALL become first-class governance records.

8. Conflict Severity

Atlas defines five canonical severity levels.

Level	Description
Informational	No operational impact
Low	Minor ambiguity
Moderate	Operational inconsistency
High	Significant governance risk
Critical	Unsafe or contradictory governance

Severity SHALL influence resolution workflows.

9. Resolution Precedence

Atlas defines the canonical precedence hierarchy.

Explicit Override Relationship
Constitutional Atlas Policies
Higher Governance Authority
Higher Policy Priority
More Specific Applicability
Newer Approved Policy Version
Domain-Specific Policy
Human Governance Decision

Each resolution SHALL record the applied precedence rule.

10. Resolution Strategies

Atlas defines the following canonical resolution strategies.

Strategy	Description
Override	Explicit replacement
Specialization	More specific policy prevails
Aggregation	Combine compatible outcomes
Delegation	Refer to another authority
Escalation	Human review required
Defer	Await additional context
Reject	Abort evaluation

Strategies SHALL be explicitly selected.

11. Conflict Resolution Workflow
Conflict Detected
        │
        ▼
Classification
        │
        ▼
Severity Assessment
        │
        ▼
Precedence Evaluation
        │
        ▼
Resolution Strategy
        │
        ▼
Decision Produced
        │
        ▼
Audit Recorded

Every step SHALL be independently traceable.

12. Escalation

Unresolved conflicts SHALL be escalated.

Escalation SHALL define:

Escalation authority
Escalation reason
Required evidence
Response deadline
Final decision authority

Escalation SHALL generate Canonical Events.

13. Resolution Record

Every conflict resolution SHALL generate an immutable resolution record containing:

Resolution ID
Participating policies
Conflict category
Severity
Resolution strategy
Applied precedence
Decision
Supporting evidence
Timestamp

Resolution records SHALL conform to the Canonical Atlas Object model.

14. Governance

Every conflict resolution SHALL define:

Responsible authority
Governance domain
Applicable policies
Approval requirements
Audit requirements
Review requirements

Governance SHALL remain independently traceable.

15. Quality Requirements

Every conflict resolution SHALL satisfy:

Determinism
Explainability
Traceability
Correctness
Consistency
Auditability
Governance compliance
Semantic preservation

Quality SHALL be continuously evaluated.

16. Conformance

A Policy Conflict Resolution implementation conforms if it:

Detects canonical conflict categories.
Applies deterministic precedence rules.
Produces governed resolution records.
Supports escalation.
Preserves policy semantics.
Maintains auditability.
Preserves complete traceability.
17. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

AI-assisted conflict analysis.
Predictive conflict detection.
Distributed conflict resolution.
Ontology-aware conflict reasoning.
Cross-enterprise conflict mediation.
Self-optimizing precedence strategies.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical conflict resolution semantics.

